Source: aurora-bless
Section: admin
Priority: optional
Maintainer: Aurora Silicon <noreply@aurorasilicon.org>
Build-Depends: debhelper-compat (= 13), cargo:native, rustc:native, libstd-rust-dev, pkgconf,
 libdbus-1-dev, ca-certificates
Standards-Version: 4.7.2
Homepage: https://github.com/aurora-silicon/aurora-bless
Rules-Requires-Root: no

Package: aurora-bless
Architecture: arm64
Depends: ${misc:Depends}, ${shlibs:Depends}
Provides: asahi-bless (= ${binary:Version})
Conflicts: asahi-bless
Replaces: asahi-bless
Description: startup-volume selector for Apple silicon Macs (Aurora)
 aurora-bless selects the volume an Apple silicon Mac starts from, like
 macOS's Startup Disk, including the MacBook Neo's NVRAM. It replaces Debian's
 asahi-bless and is also installed under that name.

Package: aurora-nvram
Architecture: arm64
Depends: ${misc:Depends}, ${shlibs:Depends}
Provides: asahi-nvram (= ${binary:Version})
Conflicts: asahi-nvram
Replaces: asahi-nvram
Description: read and write Apple silicon NVRAM variables (Aurora)
 aurora-nvram reads and writes the NVRAM variables of Apple silicon Macs,
 including the MacBook Neo's NVRAM. It replaces Debian's asahi-nvram and is
 also installed under that name.

Package: aurora-btsync
Architecture: arm64
Depends: bluez, dbus, systemd, ${misc:Depends}, ${shlibs:Depends}
Provides: asahi-btsync (= ${binary:Version})
Conflicts: asahi-btsync
Replaces: asahi-btsync
Description: use the Bluetooth pairings made in macOS on Linux (Aurora)
 aurora-btsync copies the Bluetooth pairing keys that macOS keeps in NVRAM
 into BlueZ's storage, so devices paired in macOS work on Linux. A udev rule
 runs it when the NVRAM device appears, before bluetoothd starts. It replaces
 Debian's asahi-btsync and is also installed under that name.

Package: aurora-panel-nits
Architecture: arm64
Depends: systemd, ${misc:Depends}, ${shlibs:Depends}
Description: panel brightness shared with the boot loader through NVRAM
 aurora-panel-nits keeps one brightness level for the built-in panel of Apple
 silicon Macs, shared by the boot loader, Linux and macOS through the NVRAM
 variable backlight-nits. Its service starts once the panel's backlight and
 the NVRAM device exist, and takes over systemd-backlight's restore for the
 built-in panel.
